        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Rebecca Abbott is an American food photographer. A year ago, she bought her first air fryer. She started with the usual air-fryer dishes, like frozen pizza rolls. But it didn’t take her long to realise that her model could make almost anything. Four months later, she was amazed to see a cheesecake come out of the machine. It was baked to perfection in just 20 minutes."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Then, the floodgates opened,” Abbott said. She thought, what else could she cook in the air fryer? Beef tenderloin, crab legs, lobster tails, and pumpkin pie—she has made them all in the appliance. Abbott now calls it her “magic box” and runs a social media group called Easy Air Fryer Recipes. The group has nearly a million members. They share recipes and tips for the appliance."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Fred van der Weij, an inventor in the Netherlands, started developing the air fryer in 2005. Before his invention, he said, making French fries at home took too long and usually produced disappointing results. His egg-shaped fryer, produced by Philips, came out in 2010. “People love "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "crispy",
            "zh": "酥脆的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " food,” said Weij. “But I didn’t expect my fryer to be as big as it is now.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Nearly 60 percent of US families now own an air fryer. Many people have turned to the appliance because it can deep-fry with little oil. But just like Abbott, they have also discovered that it can cook more than just crispy food. An air fryer can make breakfast, lunch, and dinner. For some cooks, it has nearly replaced the oven or microwave. “Getting an air fryer isn’t about making fries,” said Megha Ramesh, a food-science student in America. “Instead, it’s about how to get the best out of the food we made yesterday.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "It is unclear how long this popularity will last. Microwaves and ovens might "
          },
          {
            "t": "underline",
            "v": "have had their day"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", as in most homes, they are now taking a break. Chinyerem Uguru, a blogger in New York, uses her air fryer daily. She has used her oven only a few times over the last six months."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
